# Forbidden patterns for Kotlin files (*.kt, *.kts). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: kt kts
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation; no literal TAB.
# Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/kotlin.txt`.
# Note: *.kts are Gradle/script files where println() is idiomatic; flagging it there
# still catches stray debug output, but expect to scaffold-allow legitimate cases.
# print/println/TODO anchors exclude a preceding `.` so member calls like writer.println(),
# document.print(), and obj.TODO() do NOT match - only the unqualified kotlin.io builtins do.

(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.])println[[:space:]]*\(	Use the project's logger (e.g. slf4j/kotlin-logging), not println()
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.])print[[:space:]]*\(	Use the project's logger, not print()
System\.(out|err)\.print(ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Use the project's logger, not System.out/System.err.print(ln)
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])printStackTrace[[:space:]]*\(	Log the exception via the project's logger, not printStackTrace()
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.])TODO[[:space:]]*\(	kotlin.TODO() throws NotImplementedError at runtime - finish the impl or open a ticket

# --- Security: JVM RCE / TLS sinks (java.txt's rules, Kotlin syntax) ---
# Kotlin reaches the same JVM sinks and has no linter, hook lint or real CI job
# here either. Every rule here was narrowed against ~18k real Kotlin files (ktor,
# kotlinx.coroutines, kotlinx.serialization, okhttp, Exposed, detekt, koin,
# leakcanary, AnkiDroid, Signal-Android, element-android, nowinandroid). The two
# sink rules below match exactly ONE line each in that corpus and both of those
# are true positives; the hostname rule matches none.
# Runtime.exec fires ONLY when the command string is built by Kotlin string
# interpolation on the same line. A constant command (`exec("sqlite3 --version")`,
# `exec(arrayOf("git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"))`) and a command assembled on an earlier
# line (`exec(cmd)`) stay quiet - the second is a deliberate miss, a line-at-a-time
# scan cannot see it.
# checkServerTrusted fires ONLY on a `fun` DECLARATION whose body is an empty `{}`
# or `= Unit` on the same line - the copy-pasted trust-all TrustManager. A real
# implementation, a `delegate.checkServerTrusted(...)` call, an
# X509TrustManagerExtensions overload and a mock stub do not fire; an empty body
# split across two lines is not seen.
# ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER is anchored to `.ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER`, so it
# matches the member reference and its import, not prose naming the antipattern.
# ObjectInputStream and setAccessible/isAccessible are NOT active rules - both sit
# in the opt-in block below, with the measurements that put them there.
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])Runtime\.getRuntime[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)[[:space:]]*\.exec[[:space:]]*\([^)]*"[^"]*\$	Runtime.exec() with an interpolated command string is a command-injection sink - use ProcessBuilder with a fixed argv
fun[[:space:]]+checkServerTrusted[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\)[^{}]*(\{[[:space:]]*\}|=[[:space:]]*Unit)	An EMPTY checkServerTrusted() accepts every certificate - that is TLS validation switched off (MITM risk); validate the chain or keep the platform TrustManager
\.ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER	ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER disables TLS hostname checking (MITM risk) - keep the default verifier

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX comment not allowed - open a ticket instead (FP-prone in comments/strings)
# @Disabled($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	Disabled JUnit5 test committed - re-enable or delete (may be intentional)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(it|i)gnore[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true	Ignored test annotation - re-enable or delete (FP-prone; matches config flags)
# !![[:space:]]*($|[^=])	Avoid the !! not-null assertion - use safe calls or requireNotNull (very FP-prone)
# GlobalScope\.	Avoid GlobalScope - use a structured-concurrency CoroutineScope (some scripts legitimately use it)
# ObjectInputStream ships OFF: the API is a real RCE sink, but nothing on the line
# says whether the bytes are untrusted. All 8 matches in the real corpus were
# legitimate - Java-serialization round-trips in tests, and reading back a blob the
# same process wrote (leakcanary, element-android's encrypted secret store). Enable
# it in a service that deserializes bytes arriving from another party; leave it off
# in a codebase that only round-trips its own data.
# (^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])ObjectInputStream[[:space:]]*\(	Java deserialization of untrusted bytes is RCE (CVE-2015-4852 class) - use JSON/protobuf with a schema
# setAccessible/isAccessible ship OFF: a design smell, not a sink, and routine in
# framework glue and test helpers - 47 matches across 29 files in the real corpus
# (kotlinx.serialization, Exposed, ktor, Signal-Android), every one of them ordinary
# reflection. `isAccessible` is also an ordinary property name, so the opt-in form
# below is anchored to `.isAccessible =` - that keeps `Room(name = "Lab", isAccessible
# = true)` and `r.copy(isAccessible = true)` quiet. Enable it only in a codebase that
# has decided against reflection outright.
# (\.setAccessible[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*true[[:space:]]*\)|\.isAccessible[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true)	setAccessible/isAccessible = true defeats access control and final-field safety - drop the reflection (off by default: routine in framework glue and tests)
